 into explicit subtasks. Counting sort is an integer sorting algorithm that works by counting the number of occurrences of each unique element in the input list. Here's how it can be done:\n\n### Subtask Decomposition:\n\n#### 1. Determine the range of the numbers in the input list.\n   - Find the minimum value.\n   - Find the maximum value.\n\n#### 2. Create a counting array.\n   - Initialize an array of zeros with a size equal to the range of the input numbers.\n\n#### 3. Populate the counting array.\n   - Traverse the input list and increment the count of each number in the counting array.\n\n#### 4. Construct the sorted list.\n   - Traverse the counting array and construct the sorted list by adding each number to the list according to its count.\n\n### Subtree Exploration (Focus on Third Subtree Exploration):\n\n#### 1. Determine the range of the numbers in the input list.\n\n   1.1 Find the minimum value in the list.\n   - Traverse the list: `[9, 6, 6, 8, 1, 4, 1, 9, 7, 0, 7, 7, 7, 1, 7, 5]`\n   - Minimum value found: `0`\n\n   1.2 Find the maximum value in the list.\n   - Traverse the list: `[9, 6, 6, 8, 1, 4, 1, 9, 7, 0, 7, 7, 7, 1, 7, 5]`\n   - Maximum value found: `9`\n\n#### 2. Create a counting array.\n\n   2.1 Calculate the range of numbers.\n   - Range = Maximum value - Minimum value + 1 = `9 - 0 + 1 = 10`\n\n  ")'

